{"role":"assistant","content":"Done. The generator now emits instant-HR corridors instead of zone-mapped ones.\n\n**The fix** — one word in `gen.py:13`: `\"value\": lo` → `\"start\": lo`, so each HR target is now `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": <low>, \"end\": <high>}`.\n\n**Why** — per the colleague's note, the lower-bound key name selects the FIT export mode: `value`/`end` is exported as a lap-HR, zone-mapped target (the watch clamps it to its own auto-detected zone edges), while `start`/`end` is exported as an instant-HR corridor (the watch shows the raw bpm Intervals computed from its stored LTHR 172). Your watch notes match zone-clamping exactly — every displayed band was a watch zone edge, and changing the watch LTHR moved the bands but never off zone edges. With the new shape, the watch should show 117–129 / 151–160 / 129–141 / 112–124 regardless of the watch's own zones.\n\n**Verification** — `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` now emits `start`/`end` on all four HR targets. I didn't push or sync anything (no external calls), so the end-to-end watch check still needs a normal push → Garmin sync.\n\n**Colleague's note** — full applied/not-applied breakdown is in `ANSWER.md`. In short: the `start`-not-`value` breakthrough was the fix; keeping `%lthr` percentages (converted by Intervals at export) was already right and stays; the `bpm` and `start_bpm` approaches matched your own failed tries. The one part I deliberately did **not** apply is the `description` text-grammar trap — it fixes web-UI step rendering, not the watch clamping, and since a choked grammar can wipe the whole workout and I can't test against the live parser from here, adding it would be unverifiable risk for an unobserved symptom."}
